**2. Wire it up in `Program.cs`:**

```csharp
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

// Adds ProblemDetails support for automatic RFC 7807 responses
builder.Services.AddProblemDetails();
// Register the custom exception handler
builder.Services.AddExceptionHandler<CustomExceptionHandler>();

var app = builder.Build();

// Must be early in the pipeline to catch all exceptions
app.UseExceptionHandler();

app.MapGet("/example/{id}", (int id) =>
{
    // Exceptions thrown here are handled globally — no try-catch needed
    throw new KeyNotFoundException($"Item {id} not found.");
});

app.Run();
```

**How it works:**

| Component | Role |
|-----------|------|
| `AddProblemDetails()` | Enables RFC 7807 content negotiation |
| `AddExceptionHandler<T>()` | Registers your custom mapper |
| `UseExceptionHandler()` | Middleware that catches unhandled exceptions and delegates to `IExceptionHandler` |

**Key design choices:**

- **500 errors suppress `exception.Message`** — avoids leaking stack traces or internals.
- **Known exceptions expose their message** — useful for client-facing validation/conflict details.
- Returns `true` from `TryHandleAsync` so the pipeline knows the exception is fully handled.

The response body will look like:

```json
{
  "type": "https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc9110#section-15.5.5",
  "title": "Not Found",
  "status": 404,
  "detail": "Item 42 not found."
}
```
